1
00:00:02,965 --> 00:00:05,588
Van Pelt found some coded files
in Bertram's office.

2
00:00:05,922 --> 00:00:06,964
It looks like a list.

3
00:00:07,590 --> 00:00:09,467
- Patrick.
- Kim.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:11,886
"Thank you for this.
I needed the company."

5
00:00:12,303 --> 00:00:13,346
"Who doesn't?"

6
00:00:13,471 --> 00:00:14,514
I'm here to make you an offer.

7
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:17,266
The bureau is willing to drop
all the charges against you...

8
00:00:17,433 --> 00:00:19,852
- if you come and work for us.
- For the FBI?

9
00:00:20,853 --> 00:00:24,232
My terms. I need you to read 'em
and sign it.

10
00:00:24,941 --> 00:00:25,983
We can work with this.

11
00:00:26,192 --> 00:00:27,860
I'm gonna give you
my home phone number

12
00:00:27,985 --> 00:00:30,112
in case you ever make it
back to the States.

13
00:00:30,863 --> 00:00:33,574
You are on U.S. soil now,
I am the federal government

14
00:00:33,699 --> 00:00:35,701
and I am telling you that this...

15
00:00:36,661 --> 00:00:39,163
is the deal.
That is a napkin.

16
00:00:39,413 --> 00:00:41,207
- Take it or leave it.
- I'll leave it.

17
00:00:43,167 --> 00:00:44,877
- Kim?
- Agent Fischer.

18
00:00:45,169 --> 00:00:46,212
Agent Fischer.

19
00:01:10,236 --> 00:01:12,530
- Have you seen the stapler?
- It busted yesterday.

20
00:01:12,863 --> 00:01:14,073
- We have another one?
- Um...

21
00:01:14,323 --> 00:01:15,449
You know, maybe try
the copying machine.

22
00:01:15,575 --> 00:01:17,618
- It has a staple function.
- Isn't that a waste of paper?

23
00:01:17,952 --> 00:01:19,161
No, we have tons of paper.

24
00:01:20,037 --> 00:01:21,247
I'm gonna go with a paper clip.

25
00:01:24,125 --> 00:01:26,711
- Agent Fischer.
- Chief Lisbon. Can we talk?

26
00:01:30,256 --> 00:01:31,299
It's adorable.

27
00:01:32,091 --> 00:01:33,259
Please. Sit down.

28
00:01:37,263 --> 00:01:39,473
- How's Jane?
- He is still in detention.

29
00:01:40,016 --> 00:01:42,351
I know.
They won't deliver my letters.

30
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:43,853
Three months now.

31
00:01:43,978 --> 00:01:45,980
He's being kept in isolation by design.

32
00:01:46,272 --> 00:01:47,315
By design?

33
00:01:48,024 --> 00:01:49,984
You mean until he does
what you want.

34
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:52,361
- We want to help him out.
- You wanna use him.

35
00:01:52,612 --> 00:01:53,654
Is that so bad?

36
00:01:54,113 --> 00:01:57,658
In case you haven't noticed
Jane is hardly federal agent material.

37
00:01:58,242 --> 00:01:59,744
Was he state agent material?

38
00:02:00,077 --> 00:02:01,996
No, not really,
but he wanted to be there.

39
00:02:02,288 --> 00:02:03,456
And while he was there

40
00:02:03,581 --> 00:02:06,167
he stopped the 10-year reign
of a mass murderer

41
00:02:06,292 --> 00:02:08,919
and exposed a society of corruption
in law enforcement

42
00:02:09,045 --> 00:02:10,755
that spread halfway
across the country.

43
00:02:10,921 --> 00:02:13,633
Yeah, that didn't exactly
go down by the books, did it?

44
00:02:13,799 --> 00:02:16,636
Yeah, we know how Jane works.
And we know he's a risk.

45
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:18,512
But we can't ignore the upside.

46
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:21,474
He closed every case
you were ever assigned.

47
00:02:21,807 --> 00:02:24,185
- Yeah, he had help.
- Which is why I'm here.

48
00:02:25,436 --> 00:02:26,479
What do you want from me?

49
00:02:26,979 --> 00:02:28,022
Help me, Lisbon.

50
00:02:28,731 --> 00:02:30,941
There are national security
issues at play.

51
00:02:31,108 --> 00:02:33,319
Jane won't work with us
unless we meet his demands.

52
00:02:33,861 --> 00:02:35,571
He has too much pride
to back down.

53
00:02:35,696 --> 00:02:38,115
You gotta give him a win.
Meet one of his demands.

54
00:02:38,574 --> 00:02:41,035
- One of his demands is you.
- Meet another one.

55
00:02:41,786 --> 00:02:42,828
We're running out of time.

56
00:02:43,496 --> 00:02:45,539
Is your work here so pressing
that you can't take a break

57
00:02:45,665 --> 00:02:46,707
to help your country?

58
00:02:48,084 --> 00:02:50,378
Hey, chief, uh,
I radioed Officer Phelps.

59
00:02:50,503 --> 00:02:52,213
He can run to Kmart
for a new stapler.

60
00:02:52,338 --> 00:02:53,839
Swingline or Stanley Bostitch?

61
00:03:11,565 --> 00:03:13,818
Visitor. Splendid.

62
00:03:14,944 --> 00:03:17,321
Not a willing one,
based on the posture.

63
00:03:18,864 --> 00:03:19,907
That's a little better.

64
00:03:21,826 --> 00:03:22,868
It's good to see you.

65
00:03:23,994 --> 00:03:25,287
- How you holding up?
- Ah...

66
00:03:26,747 --> 00:03:28,791
You know
what incarceration's like.

67
00:03:29,875 --> 00:03:30,918
Not really.

68
00:03:31,043 --> 00:03:32,253
Well, there's no need
to pretend, Lisbon.

69
00:03:32,378 --> 00:03:33,671
We're both
in the same predicament.

70
00:03:33,963 --> 00:03:36,716
How is that? I have a home
and a respected career.

71
00:03:37,049 --> 00:03:38,384
You're locked up for murder.

72
00:03:38,634 --> 00:03:41,095
Eh, you're splitting hairs.
We're both trapped.

73
00:03:41,303 --> 00:03:42,805
How am I trapped?

74
00:03:43,139 --> 00:03:44,306
You live in Washington.

75
00:03:44,598 --> 00:03:46,350
- I love Washington.
- High voice.

76
00:03:50,771 --> 00:03:51,939
Why are you being
so stubborn?

77
00:03:52,356 --> 00:03:54,734
The FBI is offering you
a "get out of jail free" card.

78
00:03:54,859 --> 00:03:56,277
Well, you make that sound
like a good thing.

79
00:03:56,402 --> 00:03:58,070
- Isn't it?
- Not on their terms.

80
00:03:58,279 --> 00:04:01,323
If I can't live on my terms
I'm in prison anyway.

81
00:04:02,158 --> 00:04:04,660
Might as well just stay
in this cozy little detention suite.

82
00:04:04,994 --> 00:04:06,954
Well, that's your call,
but not today.

83
00:04:07,163 --> 00:04:08,456
The FBI dragged me here

84
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:11,083
and they're not gonna let me go home
until you help them with a case.

85
00:04:11,667 --> 00:04:13,627
So get your ass out of the chair
and let's go.

86
00:04:17,214 --> 00:04:18,257
Okay...

87
00:04:20,593 --> 00:04:23,721
Federal officers, coming through.
Thank you.

88
00:04:54,249 --> 00:04:55,876
{\an8}I don't see a couch anywhere.

89
00:04:56,001 --> 00:04:58,045
{\an8}Don't start trouble.
At least not yet.

90
00:04:58,629 --> 00:05:00,839
{\an8}"Security of our national banking system."

91
00:05:01,298 --> 00:05:03,759
{\an8}"We'll be assisting our
New York office on this case."

92
00:05:04,718 --> 00:05:07,471
{\an8}And our asset has just arrived.

93
00:05:08,472 --> 00:05:09,973
{\an8}Mr. Jane. Lisbon.

94
00:05:10,682 --> 00:05:12,601
{\an8}You called me an asset.
That's so touching.

95
00:05:13,185 --> 00:05:14,228
{\an8}I'm an optimist.

96
00:05:14,937 --> 00:05:16,688
{\an8}We just started briefing,
if you'll have a seat.

97
00:05:17,189 --> 00:05:18,941
{\an8}- I'm fine. Hey, Cho.
- Hey, Jane.

98
00:05:19,233 --> 00:05:20,275
{\an8}What's going on?

99
00:05:20,526 --> 00:05:21,777
- Hey.
- We're in the middle of a briefing.

100
00:05:21,902 --> 00:05:24,571
{\an8}- We can catch up later.
- Gotcha. Hey there.

101
00:05:30,828 --> 00:05:32,079
{\an8}This is Abel Schneiderman.

102
00:05:32,412 --> 00:05:34,957
{\an8}He led the team
that wrote the new source code

103
00:05:35,082 --> 00:05:37,417
{\an8}to protect the federal reserve
banking system.

104
00:05:38,210 --> 00:05:40,796
{\an8}Two days ago, he disappeared
from his loft in Brooklyn.

105
00:05:42,047 --> 00:05:44,007
There were signs of a struggle
in his bedroom.

106
00:05:44,132 --> 00:05:47,010
{\an8}Schneiderman's cell phone
was busted on the floor.

107
00:05:47,594 --> 00:05:49,888
{\an8}We are working on the theory
that he has been kidnapped.

108
00:05:50,222 --> 00:05:53,225
{\an8}- Has there been a ransom demand?
- Not yet, but we're expecting it.

109
00:05:53,809 --> 00:05:56,562
{\an8}Well, it's either that, or they will
break him for the code

110
00:05:56,687 --> 00:05:59,231
and launch an attack on the
Federal Reserve computer system.

111
00:06:00,148 --> 00:06:04,069
{\an8}What Abel knows could bring
our banking system to its knees.

112
00:06:04,736 --> 00:06:06,864
Oh. This is exciting.
Is this the case that I'm here for?

113
00:06:07,114 --> 00:06:09,700
- We're getting to you.
- I knew you saw me. Huh?

114
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:11,243
I knew it.

115
00:06:12,411 --> 00:06:14,955
{\an8}Abel has a young wife...
Defiance Schneiderman.

116
00:06:15,956 --> 00:06:17,541
{\an8}Now understandably, she's emotional

117
00:06:17,666 --> 00:06:20,377
{\an8}and believes that the FBI
is not utilizing

118
00:06:20,502 --> 00:06:22,796
{\an8}all of its resources
to find her husband.

119
00:06:22,921 --> 00:06:25,716
{\an8}And unfortunately, we can't let
anyone outside the FBI know

120
00:06:25,841 --> 00:06:27,801
{\an8}that Mr. Schneiderman's
been kidnapped.

121
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:30,470
Otherwise, it could cripple
the financial systems.

122
00:06:30,596 --> 00:06:33,849
The New York office sent a FBI agent
posing as a psychic

123
00:06:34,308 --> 00:06:36,602
but Ms. Schneiderman
saw right through that.

124
00:06:37,185 --> 00:06:38,854
Well, of course.
She's a Gypsy.

125
00:06:39,730 --> 00:06:41,607
Woman knows a thing or two
about psychics.

126
00:06:41,857 --> 00:06:44,443
{\an8}- What makes you say Gypsy?
- Well, Defiance is a Gypsy name.

127
00:06:44,943 --> 00:06:47,237
{\an8}Look at her bedroom.
It's Gypsy catnip.

128
00:06:48,363 --> 00:06:50,324
{\an8}Abel there is Jewish,
which means she turned her back

129
00:06:50,449 --> 00:06:52,117
{\an8}on her entire clan to get married.

130
00:06:52,367 --> 00:06:54,661
Now she has a missing husband
and no family for support.

131
00:06:54,786 --> 00:06:56,538
It's a psychic 911.

132
00:06:57,789 --> 00:06:58,999
{\an8}Well... there we are.

133
00:07:00,792 --> 00:07:01,835
{\an8}Let's go to work.

134
00:07:13,221 --> 00:07:14,264
{\an8}Thank you.

135
00:07:14,681 --> 00:07:17,768
Coach. I was expecting a federal jet
or at least a helicopter.

136
00:07:18,894 --> 00:07:19,937
Call the aisle seat.

137
00:07:20,562 --> 00:07:21,730
You'll sit where you're told.

138
00:07:22,314 --> 00:07:23,357
You know, you can relax.

139
00:07:24,191 --> 00:07:25,233
Everything's good.

140
00:07:25,984 --> 00:07:28,445
{\an8}You're still a federal detainee
and you're in my charge

141
00:07:28,570 --> 00:07:29,863
so, no, I won't relax.

142
00:07:30,447 --> 00:07:32,324
And you'll do as I tell you,
you understand?

143
00:07:32,824 --> 00:07:34,076
{\an8}Oh, I understand you, Kim.

144
00:07:34,451 --> 00:07:37,371
{\an8}I understand you very well...
better than you do yourself.

145
00:07:37,913 --> 00:07:41,708
You will stay within arm's length
of FBI personnel at all times.

146
00:07:43,168 --> 00:07:45,587
Any foolishness,
and you go back to detention.

147
00:07:47,589 --> 00:07:49,174
{\an8}She wasn't at all like this
on the island.

148
00:07:49,549 --> 00:07:51,677
- How was she on the island?
- I was undercover.

149
00:07:53,011 --> 00:07:55,847
- You mean you were lying.
- I was using a false persona.

150
00:07:55,973 --> 00:07:59,351
Actually, I don't think so.
I think that was the real you.

151
00:07:59,977 --> 00:08:03,689
I think this federal she-wolf shtick
is in fact, a false persona.

152
00:08:04,398 --> 00:08:05,732
No. This is the real me.

153
00:08:07,067 --> 00:08:08,110
We're boarding.

154
00:08:09,861 --> 00:08:10,904
Remember the rules.

155
00:08:22,499 --> 00:08:23,542
Ahh.

156
00:08:24,084 --> 00:08:26,503
I love the smell of reclaimed meat
and onion water.

157
00:08:29,172 --> 00:08:30,215
Do you have a 20?

158
00:08:32,843 --> 00:08:33,885
Thank you.

159
00:08:37,681 --> 00:08:39,725
Liaison with the New York office
is waiting upstairs.

160
00:08:39,850 --> 00:08:41,309
You two, stay here
and cover the exits.

161
00:08:41,435 --> 00:08:42,894
There are two...
west and south.

162
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:44,646
Detain anyone who tries
to leave the building.

163
00:08:46,940 --> 00:08:47,983
Hey.

164
00:08:48,859 --> 00:08:50,027
Arm's length, remember?

165
00:08:50,736 --> 00:08:51,778
Yeah. I'm just a little hungry.

166
00:08:52,195 --> 00:08:53,238
I don't care.

167
00:08:54,281 --> 00:08:55,615
- I've already paid.
- Now.

168
00:09:03,123 --> 00:09:04,291
This is where
the Schneidermans live.

169
00:09:05,125 --> 00:09:06,752
It's mostly an abandoned
storage building

170
00:09:06,877 --> 00:09:08,295
but there are some renters
on the top floor.

171
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:11,048
- No security cameras.
- What kind of renters?

172
00:09:11,423 --> 00:09:14,009
Usual fringe types...
artists, dropouts.

173
00:09:14,301 --> 00:09:16,428
These aren't legal dwellings,
but they got a hell of a view.

174
00:09:17,846 --> 00:09:19,097
In the photos you sent

175
00:09:19,222 --> 00:09:20,891
there were signs of a struggle
in the bedroom?

176
00:09:21,266 --> 00:09:22,392
That's this way.
I'll show you.

177
00:09:32,861 --> 00:09:33,904
Hi.

178
00:09:35,906 --> 00:09:36,948
Mrs. Schneiderman?

179
00:09:39,743 --> 00:09:40,786
Defiance.

180
00:09:41,328 --> 00:09:42,370
Defiance.

181
00:09:43,705 --> 00:09:44,748
You are Romani Gypsy?

182
00:09:45,832 --> 00:09:46,875
What do you know about it?

183
00:09:47,334 --> 00:09:49,628
Carny circuit.
My dad was a showman.

184
00:09:51,546 --> 00:09:54,007
So, Defiance. Yeah.

185
00:09:54,883 --> 00:09:55,926
Quite the painter.

186
00:09:59,221 --> 00:10:01,556
- And who are you?
- Patrick Jane.

187
00:10:02,432 --> 00:10:04,601
I was invited by the FBI
they said you need help.

188
00:10:10,190 --> 00:10:11,233
You're a psychic.

189
00:10:11,983 --> 00:10:13,026
So I'm told.

190
00:10:15,278 --> 00:10:16,321
All right.

191
00:10:20,408 --> 00:10:21,451
Read my palm.

192
00:10:23,203 --> 00:10:24,788
It only works
with a dominant hand.

193
00:10:30,544 --> 00:10:31,962
No. Can't do it.

194
00:10:32,963 --> 00:10:35,674
- I knew you were a fake.
- No, you're not letting me in.

195
00:10:36,341 --> 00:10:38,301
You're anxious
about finding your husband

196
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:41,471
and you don't wanna be read
by a gorger from the government.

197
00:10:41,596 --> 00:10:44,766
You're feeling scared and alone
and you don't trust strangers.

198
00:10:45,308 --> 00:10:47,227
And I don't blame you.
You can't trust these guys.

199
00:10:47,394 --> 00:10:49,187
- Jane.
- I mean, they mean well

200
00:10:49,980 --> 00:10:52,941
and they'll find your husband
if you let them do their thing.

201
00:10:53,483 --> 00:10:55,527
- Says you.
- Yes, says me.

202
00:10:55,652 --> 00:10:58,780
Why are you asking the FBI
to meet your spiritual needs?

203
00:10:59,573 --> 00:11:01,324
Don't you have a trusted psychic
of your own?

204
00:11:01,658 --> 00:11:03,160
I do. Cleo.

205
00:11:03,618 --> 00:11:05,328
But he's not answering
my phone calls.

206
00:11:05,620 --> 00:11:07,581
Hmm. That is odd.

207
00:11:08,331 --> 00:11:10,542
Can you find him?
Can you find Abel?

208
00:11:10,792 --> 00:11:12,210
Where was the last place
you saw him?

209
00:11:12,669 --> 00:11:14,546
On the roof, night before last.

210
00:11:14,754 --> 00:11:15,881
What were you doing up there?

211
00:11:16,298 --> 00:11:18,508
Sharing a bottle of wine
with my girlfriends.

212
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:21,720
Abel came up there
with his laptop.

213
00:11:22,262 --> 00:11:25,015
He likes to unwind on the roof.
It's peaceful.

214
00:11:26,349 --> 00:11:27,809
I'd like to take a look
at this roof.

215
00:11:33,231 --> 00:11:35,358
It's the stairs
at the end of the hallway.

216
00:11:38,570 --> 00:11:39,613
Hi.

217
00:11:40,906 --> 00:11:41,948
What are you doing?

218
00:11:42,574 --> 00:11:43,617
Spyhole went dark.

219
00:11:49,623 --> 00:11:50,665
What?

220
00:11:50,790 --> 00:11:51,875
We're with the FBI.

221
00:11:52,125 --> 00:11:54,085
Sir, did you see or hear
anything unusual

222
00:11:54,211 --> 00:11:55,253
In the last couple weeks?

223
00:11:55,962 --> 00:11:58,632
- I already told you people, no.
- We're with a different office.

224
00:11:59,549 --> 00:12:01,301
So many, like roaches.

225
00:12:03,929 --> 00:12:04,971
Nice guy.

226
00:12:05,472 --> 00:12:07,849
His name is Nguyen Hai.
He's a Vietnamese refugee.

227
00:12:07,974 --> 00:12:10,393
He used to run a noodle shop.
Now he owns the building.

228
00:12:10,518 --> 00:12:12,312
He was home the evening
of the disappearance

229
00:12:12,437 --> 00:12:14,648
- but he didn't hear or see anything.
- How do you know that?

230
00:12:15,565 --> 00:12:16,900
I read the case files.

231
00:12:17,025 --> 00:12:18,652
I thought you weren't interested
in the case.

232
00:12:18,902 --> 00:12:19,945
It was a long plane ride.

233
00:12:28,995 --> 00:12:31,248
So if Abel came up here
the night he disappeared

234
00:12:31,373 --> 00:12:32,707
why was the bedroom trashed?

235
00:12:33,458 --> 00:12:36,211
I don't know.
I got home late from the bar.

236
00:12:36,836 --> 00:12:40,423
When I got in, Abel was gone,
and our room was wrecked.

237
00:12:40,966 --> 00:12:42,092
Was he expecting any visitors?

238
00:12:43,301 --> 00:12:44,344
I don't think so.

239
00:12:45,679 --> 00:12:46,930
He would've told me
before I left.

240
00:12:48,056 --> 00:12:49,391
This is your husband's garden.

241
00:12:51,518 --> 00:12:54,062
Yeah. How'd you know?

242
00:12:55,480 --> 00:12:56,523
His energy's here.

243
00:12:57,399 --> 00:12:58,733
He pours his energy
into this garden

244
00:12:58,858 --> 00:13:00,986
because he doesn't like
being a computer programmer.

245
00:13:01,319 --> 00:13:02,362
That's right.

246
00:13:02,904 --> 00:13:04,030
He feels trapped in that world.

247
00:13:05,156 --> 00:13:06,199
Yes.

248
00:13:07,450 --> 00:13:10,245
It's an orderly world
full of rules and numbers.

249
00:13:11,079 --> 00:13:12,289
But it's not Abel's world.

250
00:13:12,998 --> 00:13:15,083
He sees himself
more as a rebel soul.

251
00:13:16,584 --> 00:13:18,586
He really loves that
Che Guevara guy.

252
00:13:18,753 --> 00:13:20,964
Yeah, that's why he rides
a motorcycle.

253
00:13:21,381 --> 00:13:22,632
Grows his own vegetables.

254
00:13:23,883 --> 00:13:24,926
That's why he married you.

255
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:27,554
- Oh, because I'm a Gypsy?
- Yeah.

256
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:30,557
Because you had the courage
to walk away

257
00:13:30,682 --> 00:13:32,684
from centuries of tradition for love.

258
00:13:34,394 --> 00:13:35,854
You may not know it,
but you give your husband

259
00:13:35,979 --> 00:13:38,189
the courage to be who he is
on the inside.

260
00:13:39,190 --> 00:13:40,400
Is Abel alive?

261
00:13:46,072 --> 00:13:47,115
Why don't you help me find him?

262
00:13:51,995 --> 00:13:54,039
- I can feel him.
- Where is he?

263
00:13:55,498 --> 00:13:57,542
Just not getting a clear read.

264
00:13:58,084 --> 00:13:59,127
What should I do?

265
00:14:01,004 --> 00:14:02,047
Well...

266
00:14:03,923 --> 00:14:05,800
don't tell them I said this

267
00:14:06,718 --> 00:14:09,471
but you should sneak off
and notify the media right away.

268
00:14:11,181 --> 00:14:13,808
So that this city can pull together
and help find him.

269
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:15,602
That is your only hope

270
00:14:16,227 --> 00:14:17,604
at saving your husband's life.

271
00:14:19,981 --> 00:14:21,232
- Okay.
- Go.

272
00:14:27,655 --> 00:14:28,698
Where's she going?

273
00:14:28,907 --> 00:14:31,368
She, uh... wants to call the press.

274
00:14:31,493 --> 00:14:33,036
I... I tried to stop her.

275
00:14:33,745 --> 00:14:34,788
Downstairs.

276
00:14:38,958 --> 00:14:40,919
You must really miss
that prison cell.

277
00:14:44,297 --> 00:14:45,340
"I'll try and talk to her."

278
00:14:45,673 --> 00:14:47,175
You have done enough talking.

279
00:14:53,681 --> 00:14:54,724
Hi.

280
00:15:27,672 --> 00:15:28,798
After Jane returned
to the roof

281
00:15:28,923 --> 00:15:30,716
he went down a fire escape
to the south alley.

282
00:15:32,927 --> 00:15:34,137
And that's where the cameras
lose sight of him.

283
00:15:34,762 --> 00:15:36,013
Who was covering
the south exit?

284
00:15:36,431 --> 00:15:39,600
I was. But I didn't have a line
of sight to the alley.

285
00:15:40,435 --> 00:15:41,853
There are no exits there,
so I didn't think...

286
00:15:41,978 --> 00:15:44,522
You misread the situation.
There'll be a note in your file.

287
00:15:46,941 --> 00:15:47,984
Yes, ma'am.

288
00:15:48,526 --> 00:15:50,528
Collins wasn't the only one
to misread the situation.

289
00:15:52,071 --> 00:15:53,281
- You mean me?
- Yeah.

290
00:15:54,991 --> 00:15:56,742
You're correct.
I screwed up, too.

291
00:15:57,702 --> 00:16:00,413
And supervising Agent Abbott
will note that in my file.

292
00:16:00,997 --> 00:16:02,039
Fair enough.

293
00:16:02,623 --> 00:16:04,917
Why don't you and Agent Collins
focus on looking for Jane?

294
00:16:05,376 --> 00:16:06,419
You have an insight there.

295
00:16:07,003 --> 00:16:08,629
Coordinate with Davis
in the New York office.

296
00:16:10,798 --> 00:16:12,091
- Yes, ma'am.
- Thank you.

297
00:16:13,301 --> 00:16:14,677
This is the real you, huh?

298
00:16:16,053 --> 00:16:17,972
Let's keep our eyes
on the prize, shall we?

299
00:16:18,473 --> 00:16:19,515
Abel Schneiderman.

300
00:16:19,682 --> 00:16:21,684
We have narrowed
his kidnapping suspects

301
00:16:21,809 --> 00:16:23,144
- to three extremist groups...
- Excuse me?

302
00:16:26,063 --> 00:16:27,106
Why am I here?

303
00:16:27,648 --> 00:16:30,234
- What do you mean?
- I have no business. Jane's gone.

304
00:16:30,943 --> 00:16:31,986
We'll find him.

305
00:16:32,612 --> 00:16:34,739
Uh, most of you will recognize
these faces.

306
00:16:34,864 --> 00:16:37,241
You're only gonna find him
if he wants to be found

307
00:16:37,366 --> 00:16:39,702
which he doesn't, or he wouldn't
have left in the first place.

308
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:42,163
Take five.

309
00:16:53,341 --> 00:16:56,344
Tell me something...
Did you help Jane escape?

310
00:16:57,970 --> 00:16:59,013
Why would I do that?

311
00:16:59,555 --> 00:17:00,973
You're h... his friend.

312
00:17:01,682 --> 00:17:04,393
Yes, I am, but I'm also
an officer of the law.

313
00:17:05,394 --> 00:17:07,146
I'm not trying to get you in trouble.
I just...

314
00:17:10,525 --> 00:17:11,567
I need your help.

315
00:17:15,238 --> 00:17:18,783
I was so certain
I had him figured out.

316
00:17:20,618 --> 00:17:22,036
Welcome to my life.

317
00:17:23,746 --> 00:17:25,581
How did you make it work
for so long?

318
00:17:27,041 --> 00:17:29,043
I've thought about that myself.

319
00:17:29,335 --> 00:17:31,420
I mean, after he disappeared.

320
00:17:32,004 --> 00:17:33,881
How I made it work for so long.

321
00:17:34,423 --> 00:17:35,466
And what's the answer?

322
00:17:36,425 --> 00:17:37,677
I have no idea.

323
00:17:39,762 --> 00:17:41,305
Were you two ever involved?

324
00:17:42,932 --> 00:17:44,350
That's a strange question.

325
00:17:46,227 --> 00:17:47,603
Why would you ask me that?

326
00:17:48,938 --> 00:17:49,981
Uh...

327
00:17:50,231 --> 00:17:51,691
No reason. I just... I...

328
00:17:54,610 --> 00:17:56,237
uh, nev... never mind.

329
00:19:20,488 --> 00:19:21,572
What are you doing?

330
00:19:22,698 --> 00:19:23,949
Waiting for you.

331
00:19:24,950 --> 00:19:25,993
To do what?

332
00:19:27,912 --> 00:19:28,954
Notice me.

333
00:19:33,501 --> 00:19:34,543
Who are you?

334
00:19:34,710 --> 00:19:38,464
Jason Wylie. Uh, junior I.T. analyst.
I work in I.T.

335
00:19:39,715 --> 00:19:41,050
Analyzing stuff.

336
00:19:41,592 --> 00:19:42,635
Okay.

337
00:19:43,469 --> 00:19:44,553
What do you want, Jason?

338
00:19:45,221 --> 00:19:47,890
Uh, we were told to focus I.T. support
on the Schneiderman case

339
00:19:48,015 --> 00:19:50,017
but I'm my own man
at lunch, right?

340
00:19:50,267 --> 00:19:53,104
So I stole some satellite time
to hunt for Patrick Jane.

341
00:19:56,440 --> 00:19:57,525
Look what I found.

342
00:20:04,365 --> 00:20:07,326
This was taken 22 minutes ago
from a satellite over Brooklyn.

343
00:20:07,451 --> 00:20:09,537
You think it's really Jane?
It's not a trick?

344
00:20:09,745 --> 00:20:12,415
- That's Jane.
- Notify New York right away.

345
00:20:14,750 --> 00:20:16,711
- Who found this?
- Analyst Wylie.

346
00:20:16,836 --> 00:20:19,255
- On his own time.
- Who is analyst Wylie?

347
00:20:20,256 --> 00:20:21,298
Right there.

348
00:20:26,679 --> 00:20:27,972
Feel free to join us.

349
00:20:34,729 --> 00:20:36,355
You took this initiative
on your own?

350
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:37,523
Yes, sir.

351
00:20:37,815 --> 00:20:39,734
Well, let's see what you can do
on the primary team.

352
00:20:40,067 --> 00:20:42,611
Stanley, find Wylie a desk
in the bullpen.

353
00:21:01,255 --> 00:21:02,882
Down!
Get down!

354
00:21:04,550 --> 00:21:06,010
Face down on the street!

355
00:21:22,943 --> 00:21:24,195
Thank you.

356
00:21:48,302 --> 00:21:49,386
Congratulations.

357
00:21:49,595 --> 00:21:51,639
You got one over on me.
It's a nice stunt.

358
00:21:52,097 --> 00:21:53,307
You feel better now?

359
00:21:53,808 --> 00:21:56,227
No. You woke me up.

360
00:21:56,560 --> 00:21:57,812
I don't get you.

361
00:21:58,270 --> 00:21:59,814
You hunted down
and killed your enemy.

362
00:21:59,939 --> 00:22:01,315
Why are you making new ones?

363
00:22:01,565 --> 00:22:02,900
Eh, you're right.

364
00:22:03,442 --> 00:22:04,985
Running away was a mistake.

365
00:22:06,695 --> 00:22:08,948
I'm ready to discuss
the job offer now.

366
00:22:09,532 --> 00:22:11,367
There is no offer, not anymore.

367
00:22:11,492 --> 00:22:13,577
Nonsense. Let's talk.

368
00:22:13,702 --> 00:22:15,287
I am taking you back to Texas.

369
00:22:16,038 --> 00:22:19,625
And next week, you'll be indicted
for the murder of Thomas McAllister

370
00:22:19,917 --> 00:22:22,002
among other charges.
Guard.

371
00:22:25,923 --> 00:22:27,591
You cats find the body yet?

372
00:22:30,177 --> 00:22:31,220
What body?

373
00:22:31,345 --> 00:22:34,431
The body hidden on the roof
of Abel Schneiderman's building.

374
00:22:35,307 --> 00:22:36,392
You saw it?

375
00:22:37,017 --> 00:22:40,521
No, but there's a large pile of soil
just by the garden.

376
00:22:41,230 --> 00:22:44,984
Winter is almost upon us
so what is that soil doing there?

377
00:22:45,234 --> 00:22:47,152
That's a rhetorical question.

378
00:22:47,403 --> 00:22:49,238
The soil is there
to make room for Cleo...

379
00:22:49,488 --> 00:22:51,156
Mrs. Schneiderman's psychic.

380
00:22:51,574 --> 00:22:53,868
He went missing
the same time as her husband.

381
00:22:53,993 --> 00:22:55,035
What are you talking about?

382
00:22:55,202 --> 00:22:57,621
Abel found out his wife
was sleeping with her psychic

383
00:22:57,746 --> 00:22:59,456
so he killed him in the bedroom.

384
00:22:59,748 --> 00:23:02,042
But Abel doesn't own a car,
so he cut up the body

385
00:23:02,167 --> 00:23:04,670
into pieces and buried them
in the garden.

386
00:23:05,713 --> 00:23:09,258
Your kidnap victim isn't kidnapped.
He's a killer.

387
00:23:09,425 --> 00:23:10,551
And he's on the run.

388
00:23:12,803 --> 00:23:13,888
You're good.

389
00:23:14,638 --> 00:23:15,806
Thank you.

390
00:23:16,140 --> 00:23:17,391
A good liar.

391
00:23:18,976 --> 00:23:20,102
Eh...

392
00:23:30,154 --> 00:23:32,239
I really hope
this isn't his idea of a joke.

393
00:23:33,365 --> 00:23:34,533
There is that chance.

394
00:23:36,243 --> 00:23:37,828
There's also the chance
that he's right.

395
00:23:40,998 --> 00:23:43,876
If there's one thing
that this job kills inside

396
00:23:44,835 --> 00:23:46,545
It's a sense of trust.

397
00:23:49,256 --> 00:23:50,966
I almost forgot what it feels like.

398
00:23:58,349 --> 00:23:59,683
Hey, let me get some light
over here.

399
00:24:09,944 --> 00:24:11,111
Well, there we go.

400
00:24:23,847 --> 00:24:24,890
Hey.

401
00:24:25,515 --> 00:24:26,558
Hey.

402
00:24:27,100 --> 00:24:28,602
I can't believe
they gave me a desk.

403
00:24:31,313 --> 00:24:33,065
You can call me The Coyote.

404
00:24:33,940 --> 00:24:34,983
I don't think so.

405
00:24:35,275 --> 00:24:36,902
That's just what they
call me downstairs.

406
00:24:37,027 --> 00:24:38,612
Don't ask me why, though.
They never told me.

407
00:24:41,865 --> 00:24:42,991
Your name's Wylie.

408
00:24:43,450 --> 00:24:44,493
Yeah.

409
00:24:47,829 --> 00:24:49,081
Wile E. Coyote.

410
00:24:49,581 --> 00:24:50,999
Road Runner, the cartoon?

411
00:24:52,167 --> 00:24:53,877
No, it's Irish, I think.

412
00:24:56,588 --> 00:24:58,340
Uh, good morning, Agent Stanley.

413
00:24:59,716 --> 00:25:00,759
Hey...

414
00:25:01,218 --> 00:25:02,969
Do you know the road runner
and the coyote?

415
00:25:03,512 --> 00:25:05,972
Yeah. Sex traffickers in Dallas.

416
00:25:09,935 --> 00:25:12,020
There's a cartoon
about sex trafficking?

417
00:25:13,355 --> 00:25:14,856
Hey, Cho, you got a minute?

418
00:25:15,565 --> 00:25:16,608
Yeah.

419
00:25:23,448 --> 00:25:25,158
Abbott and I went
to Brooklyn last night.

420
00:25:25,283 --> 00:25:27,828
There was a body hidden on the roof.
Jane was right.

421
00:25:27,953 --> 00:25:30,664
- He usually is. You get an ID?
- We're waiting on forensics

422
00:25:30,789 --> 00:25:32,791
but it appears to be
Mrs. Schneiderman's psychic

423
00:25:32,916 --> 00:25:34,167
just like Jane said.

424
00:25:34,876 --> 00:25:36,670
The director wants me
to bring him back on the case.

425
00:25:36,920 --> 00:25:37,963
That's a good call.

426
00:25:38,171 --> 00:25:39,506
He made fools of us.

427
00:25:39,798 --> 00:25:41,216
It won't be the last time.

428
00:25:42,342 --> 00:25:43,760
How do I get him under control?

429
00:25:44,594 --> 00:25:46,888
You don't.
Jane is what he is.

430
00:25:47,055 --> 00:25:49,349
He'll use you to turn every situation
to his advantage.

431
00:25:49,891 --> 00:25:52,894
- So what would you suggest I do?
- Use him to your advantage.

432
00:25:59,526 --> 00:26:01,611
- Found the body?
- Yes.

433
00:26:02,070 --> 00:26:05,490
Cleo Ascencio, New York psychic
astrologer, healer.

434
00:26:05,949 --> 00:26:08,493
Also found a hacksaw
hidden in the planters

435
00:26:08,618 --> 00:26:10,203
same brand as the other tools
in Abel's kit

436
00:26:10,328 --> 00:26:12,038
with traces of the victim's blood.

437
00:26:12,372 --> 00:26:14,499
So Abel killed his wife's lover
and ran away

438
00:26:14,624 --> 00:26:17,043
just... like I said.

439
00:26:17,460 --> 00:26:19,087
Well, it appears so.

440
00:26:19,838 --> 00:26:21,506
Oh. Don't beat yourselves up.

441
00:26:21,631 --> 00:26:23,216
You would've figured it out
eventually.

442
00:26:24,467 --> 00:26:26,845
I said, "appears so."

443
00:26:27,262 --> 00:26:28,763
He just received a ransom note.

444
00:26:29,472 --> 00:26:32,851
A third-class letter sent directly
to Abel Schneiderman's office.

445
00:26:32,976 --> 00:26:35,270
What kinda kidnapper
uses snail mail?

446
00:26:35,395 --> 00:26:36,688
It's smart, really.

447
00:26:37,147 --> 00:26:40,567
Low-tech, old school,
no electronic footprint.

448
00:26:40,692 --> 00:26:43,528
Unidentified kidnappers
want $10 million

449
00:26:43,653 --> 00:26:45,655
deposited into an overseas
account by midnight

450
00:26:45,780 --> 00:26:48,033
or they will sell Schneiderman
to the highest bidder.

451
00:26:48,283 --> 00:26:50,619
Next step would be a cyber-attack
on the Federal Reserve.

452
00:26:50,744 --> 00:26:52,662
- You want me to notify secret service?
- Please do.

453
00:26:54,247 --> 00:26:55,415
This isn't right.

454
00:26:55,957 --> 00:26:57,375
There are no kidnappers.

455
00:26:58,460 --> 00:26:59,711
What am I missing?

456
00:26:59,920 --> 00:27:01,671
Maybe Abel faked
his own kidnapping

457
00:27:01,796 --> 00:27:03,215
to get money
to escape the country.

458
00:27:03,340 --> 00:27:05,508
That's very good, Lisbon.
That is the best theory yet

459
00:27:05,634 --> 00:27:07,135
but it's wrong, too.

460
00:27:07,260 --> 00:27:08,970
I need another look at that loft.

461
00:27:09,221 --> 00:27:11,848
Really?
Back to Brooklyn again?

462
00:27:12,098 --> 00:27:13,141
Yeah.

463
00:27:18,063 --> 00:27:20,482
- Okay.
- Don't worry. I won't escape.

464
00:27:20,607 --> 00:27:22,817
Oh, I'm not worried.
If you walk away this time...

465
00:27:22,943 --> 00:27:25,612
I know. I'll end up spending
the rest of my life in prison.

466
00:27:25,820 --> 00:27:28,031
No. I'll shoot you.

467
00:27:36,248 --> 00:27:37,999
Oh, look, they have Skymall.

468
00:27:40,293 --> 00:27:41,503
Skymall.

469
00:27:56,559 --> 00:27:58,186
Okay, Lisbon, what's the matter?

470
00:27:58,687 --> 00:27:59,729
You.

471
00:28:00,021 --> 00:28:01,564
Was it that comment
I made in the bullpen?

472
00:28:01,690 --> 00:28:03,400
Because I think
it's a really good theory.

473
00:28:03,650 --> 00:28:05,277
I don't care about that.

474
00:28:06,403 --> 00:28:07,904
You ran away again, Jane.

475
00:28:08,405 --> 00:28:10,323
Not from the FBI
you ran away from me.

476
00:28:10,448 --> 00:28:11,616
Yeah, but I ran back.

477
00:28:11,908 --> 00:28:13,201
How was I supposed to know?

478
00:28:13,493 --> 00:28:15,328
I thought you were gone
again forever.

479
00:28:25,463 --> 00:28:27,173
Okay, you're right.
I... I'm sorry, Lisbon.

480
00:28:27,299 --> 00:28:28,967
I... I didn't think about you.

481
00:28:29,092 --> 00:28:30,593
Well, you rarely do.

482
00:28:30,802 --> 00:28:32,345
Well, that's not true.

483
00:28:33,847 --> 00:28:36,266
I made you one of my demands.
I'm... I'm not joining the FBI

484
00:28:36,391 --> 00:28:38,018
unless they make you
a job offer.

485
00:28:38,143 --> 00:28:39,185
That's my point.

486
00:28:40,395 --> 00:28:42,731
What makes you think
I wanna work with you again?

487
00:28:43,189 --> 00:28:44,816
You are difficult and exhausting

488
00:28:44,941 --> 00:28:47,319
and maybe I don't wanna put
the rest of my life on hold

489
00:28:47,444 --> 00:28:48,653
to be your sidekick.

490
00:28:49,070 --> 00:28:50,530
Have you ever even thought
about that?

491
00:28:50,905 --> 00:28:52,073
No... No.

492
00:28:52,490 --> 00:28:53,825
I hadn't considered it.

493
00:28:54,200 --> 00:28:56,494
You think you know
what's good for my life

494
00:28:56,619 --> 00:28:59,372
but you haven't been a part
of my life for two years.

495
00:29:03,710 --> 00:29:06,338
So let's get this case wrapped up
so I can go home.

496
00:29:09,382 --> 00:29:11,551
How dare you think
that I would cheat on Abel!

497
00:29:11,676 --> 00:29:12,719
And with Cleo?

498
00:29:13,762 --> 00:29:16,639
No, I... I wasn't reading you.
I was reading your husband.

499
00:29:16,765 --> 00:29:18,850
You're a catch.
Your husband's a computer nerd

500
00:29:18,975 --> 00:29:21,394
pretending to be a rebel
to try and hang on to you.

501
00:29:21,936 --> 00:29:24,314
Men like that snap
when they feel betrayed.

502
00:29:24,647 --> 00:29:27,901
It's true.
Abel is very jealous

503
00:29:28,026 --> 00:29:30,195
and insecure
and he overcompensates

504
00:29:30,320 --> 00:29:32,530
but you missed one detail.

505
00:29:34,366 --> 00:29:35,408
You're in love.

506
00:29:35,700 --> 00:29:37,077
He's my little lion

507
00:29:37,369 --> 00:29:39,996
and I would never sleep
with another man.

508
00:29:40,372 --> 00:29:41,539
I see that now.

509
00:29:45,585 --> 00:29:48,505
I would like to be alone.
Please leave.

510
00:29:49,631 --> 00:29:50,673
Okay.

511
00:29:52,467 --> 00:29:54,260
Do you still think
Abel killed Cleo?

512
00:29:54,427 --> 00:29:56,429
Oh, I know he did.
Guy's a hothead.

513
00:29:56,596 --> 00:29:58,807
Those swollen biceps
and neck tattoos.

514
00:29:59,224 --> 00:30:01,810
The mere hint of an affair
would set that guy off.

515
00:30:02,143 --> 00:30:04,437
Why was her psychic here
the night Abel disappeared?

516
00:30:04,562 --> 00:30:05,980
Defiance was out
with her friends all night.

517
00:30:06,106 --> 00:30:07,982
There was no reason for him
to be at the loft.

518
00:30:08,400 --> 00:30:11,653
Cleo Ascencio.
It's such a theatrical name.

519
00:30:11,778 --> 00:30:14,280
- You think it's real?
- It is. We ran his fingerprints.

520
00:30:14,489 --> 00:30:16,574
- Let me guess. Clean record?
- Spotless.

521
00:30:16,825 --> 00:30:18,284
What are you suggesting, Lisbon?

522
00:30:18,410 --> 00:30:20,328
Ascencio...
it sounds Cuban.

523
00:30:20,453 --> 00:30:22,705
There's a scam
that they do in Cuba.

524
00:30:22,956 --> 00:30:26,126
Brokers charge large sums of money
to surgically modify fingerprints.

525
00:30:26,376 --> 00:30:28,294
They swap the right fingertips
and the left.

526
00:30:28,420 --> 00:30:30,255
It fools immigration every time.

527
00:30:31,297 --> 00:30:34,175
- How do you know that?
- I'm a police chief.

528
00:30:37,887 --> 00:30:39,848
With a lot of reading time
on my hands.

529
00:30:41,850 --> 00:30:43,309
I did what chief Lisbon suggested

530
00:30:43,435 --> 00:30:45,562
and ran the left-hand fingerprints
for the right

531
00:30:46,187 --> 00:30:47,230
and it worked.

532
00:30:47,814 --> 00:30:50,525
Cleo Ascencio transforms into...

533
00:30:51,609 --> 00:30:53,278
Jose Martinez.

534
00:30:53,528 --> 00:30:55,363
- And who is he?
- A fraud.

535
00:30:55,488 --> 00:30:56,573
A street hustler from Havana.

536
00:30:56,865 --> 00:30:59,451
Made a living picking pockets
until he stole a wallet

537
00:30:59,576 --> 00:31:01,119
from one of Castro's ministers.

538
00:31:01,286 --> 00:31:02,787
Now he's wanted by the P.N.R.

539
00:31:09,502 --> 00:31:11,004
Jose Martinez?

540
00:31:11,254 --> 00:31:13,089
He fled Cuba and set up shop here.

541
00:31:13,339 --> 00:31:14,924
Okay, so he changed
his name.

542
00:31:15,425 --> 00:31:16,885
That doesn't mean
he's a fraud.

543
00:31:17,093 --> 00:31:18,636
You have any idea
why he was in your loft

544
00:31:18,761 --> 00:31:19,888
the night your husband
went missing?

545
00:31:20,054 --> 00:31:22,348
Sure. Uh, Cleo would
drop by sometimes

546
00:31:22,474 --> 00:31:24,392
when he thought he had
an insight I should hear.

547
00:31:24,726 --> 00:31:27,645
Now, if you weren't here, would Abel
let him into the building?

548
00:31:27,896 --> 00:31:30,148
He wouldn't have to.
Cleo had the security code.

549
00:31:30,273 --> 00:31:31,441
He would just buzz himself in.

550
00:31:31,566 --> 00:31:34,652
Well, that explains what he was doing
in your bedroom two nights ago.

551
00:31:35,320 --> 00:31:36,821
- It does?
- Hot reading.

552
00:31:37,238 --> 00:31:39,782
It's the bread and butter
of the spiritual flimflam man.

553
00:31:40,533 --> 00:31:42,827
You gather personal effects
on your subject in advance

554
00:31:42,952 --> 00:31:46,331
and then you present them
as psychic revelations.

555
00:31:46,539 --> 00:31:48,666
Martinez came up to see
if Defiance wanted a reading.

556
00:31:49,417 --> 00:31:51,169
But she was out drinking,
and Abel was on the roof.

557
00:31:51,294 --> 00:31:54,130
So he came up into the house
and he took a little snoop.

558
00:31:54,255 --> 00:31:55,590
When Abel came downstairs

559
00:31:55,882 --> 00:31:57,133
he caught your psychic
in the bedroom

560
00:31:57,258 --> 00:31:58,843
and assumed you were
sleeping with him.

561
00:32:00,345 --> 00:32:02,180
Okay. Fine.

562
00:32:03,389 --> 00:32:06,267
But none of that explains
who took Abel from the building.

563
00:32:06,601 --> 00:32:10,146
No, it doesn't, because I don't think
Abel ever left the building.

564
00:32:10,271 --> 00:32:12,023
If my suspicion is correct

565
00:32:12,273 --> 00:32:15,360
he is still here,
and he is still very much alive.

566
00:32:24,075 --> 00:32:26,077
One of us needs to stay here
with Defiance.

567
00:32:26,827 --> 00:32:28,579
- I'll stay.
- You don't want in on the bust?

568
00:32:28,788 --> 00:32:31,082
Nah, the three of us have done
a lotta busts together. You go.

569
00:32:31,249 --> 00:32:33,125
There's nothing like
a takedown with Jane.

570
00:32:34,669 --> 00:32:35,711
Shucks.

571
00:32:36,754 --> 00:32:37,838
Shall we?

572
00:32:47,807 --> 00:32:48,849
One...

573
00:32:50,017 --> 00:32:51,060
Two...

574
00:32:51,477 --> 00:32:52,603
Three.

575
00:32:53,938 --> 00:32:55,439
- Do you know what time it is?
- See?

576
00:32:55,565 --> 00:32:56,983
Distractible and paranoid by nature.

577
00:32:57,108 --> 00:32:59,986
Can't let anyone pass this door
without knowing who it is.

578
00:33:01,737 --> 00:33:03,114
What the hell is going on
out here?

579
00:33:03,239 --> 00:33:05,032
- Glad you asked.
- There's been a murder.

580
00:33:05,908 --> 00:33:07,660
And you said already.
Schneiderman.

581
00:33:07,868 --> 00:33:10,454
No, he was kidnapped.
Another man's been murdered.

582
00:33:10,580 --> 00:33:12,290
But you know that, right?

583
00:33:12,582 --> 00:33:13,749
I don't know
what you're talking about.

584
00:33:13,916 --> 00:33:15,126
Sure, you do.
You know everything

585
00:33:15,251 --> 00:33:17,420
including where Schneiderman
is right now.

586
00:33:17,962 --> 00:33:19,589
I don't know anything.

587
00:33:19,714 --> 00:33:21,257
He's in your apartment, isn't he?

588
00:33:23,509 --> 00:33:26,387
Probably in the bathroom.
No. You value your privacy.

589
00:33:26,512 --> 00:33:29,974
I'm gonna say...
stashed in a closet.

590
00:33:31,142 --> 00:33:32,184
Watch out, Jane.

591
00:33:33,060 --> 00:33:34,353
Open the door, Nguyen!

592
00:33:34,729 --> 00:33:36,230
Uh, somebody gonna break
the door?

593
00:33:37,315 --> 00:33:38,357
Go ahead.

594
00:33:38,941 --> 00:33:40,401
All right, I'm ready.

595
00:33:42,111 --> 00:33:43,613
- Break right.
- Copy that.

596
00:34:07,011 --> 00:34:08,971
I got him.
Go find Schneiderman.

597
00:34:23,653 --> 00:34:25,488
So tell me, Abel

598
00:34:25,946 --> 00:34:29,158
did you invite Jose Martinez
into your loft the night of his death?

599
00:34:29,283 --> 00:34:31,243
No. I came down from the roof

600
00:34:31,369 --> 00:34:32,787
and he was there
in our bedroom.

601
00:34:32,995 --> 00:34:34,163
And what was he doing?

602
00:34:34,747 --> 00:34:36,082
That's what I wanted to know.

603
00:34:40,753 --> 00:34:41,796
"Hey!"

604
00:34:42,713 --> 00:34:44,382
"Hey! Abel!"

605
00:34:45,549 --> 00:34:46,967
"Uh, this isn't what it looks like."

606
00:34:47,259 --> 00:34:49,845
- "What are you doing?"
- "Uh... nothing. See?"

607
00:34:49,970 --> 00:34:51,931
"Where's Defiance?
Defiance!"

608
00:34:52,056 --> 00:34:54,767
"She's not here.
I let myself in! I should go."

609
00:34:55,017 --> 00:34:56,644
"You're not going anywhere!"

610
00:34:57,812 --> 00:34:59,397
"I'm sorry, okay?
I should go."

611
00:35:00,731 --> 00:35:02,108
"I kinda lost it."

612
00:35:09,198 --> 00:35:11,242
"His foot got caught
in the shag throw."

613
00:35:11,367 --> 00:35:13,035
"His... his head hit
the coffee table."

614
00:35:15,037 --> 00:35:16,205
"It was horrible."

615
00:35:18,040 --> 00:35:19,583
And why didn't you call
the police?

616
00:35:19,792 --> 00:35:21,168
With a dead man on the floor?

617
00:35:21,293 --> 00:35:23,379
What you described
is involuntary manslaughter.

618
00:35:23,504 --> 00:35:24,839
You would've been out
in six months.

619
00:35:25,172 --> 00:35:26,215
Then what?

620
00:35:26,340 --> 00:35:28,759
I work the feds.
My career would've been over.

621
00:35:29,468 --> 00:35:31,011
I would've lost Defiance.

622
00:35:32,638 --> 00:35:35,057
That's when I decided
to hide the body.

623
00:35:39,979 --> 00:35:41,564
"And the squeaky
hallway door made sure"

624
00:35:41,689 --> 00:35:44,150
"you always heard
the Schneidermans."

625
00:35:46,110 --> 00:35:49,029
"That is how you came to see Abel
hauling a body to the roof."

626
00:35:53,284 --> 00:35:54,368
"Who is he?"

627
00:35:59,623 --> 00:36:01,292
"Just somebody
who deserved what he got."

628
00:36:02,084 --> 00:36:04,211
"This...
This is a bad dream."

629
00:36:04,962 --> 00:36:06,630
"Just go back to bed,
Mr. Nguyen."

630
00:36:07,423 --> 00:36:08,674
"I'm not sleepy."

631
00:36:10,760 --> 00:36:11,844
"What do you want?"

632
00:36:12,261 --> 00:36:13,763
"You want money? Fine."

633
00:36:13,971 --> 00:36:15,473
"I'll give you $5,000"

634
00:36:15,598 --> 00:36:17,349
"to forget everything
you saw here tonight."

635
00:36:18,768 --> 00:36:22,062
"You're that security contractor
for the federal government."

636
00:36:23,731 --> 00:36:24,774
"Yeah."

637
00:36:29,195 --> 00:36:31,781
"Exactly how much are you worth
to the government?"

638
00:36:37,495 --> 00:36:39,997
Our colleague Mr. Jane suspected
there was more to you

639
00:36:40,122 --> 00:36:41,207
than meets the eye.

640
00:36:41,332 --> 00:36:43,459
You didn't get those scars on your face
from running a noodle shop.

641
00:36:43,834 --> 00:36:46,754
You're a gangster from way back.
Green Dragons?

642
00:36:51,842 --> 00:36:53,135
"Born To Kill".

643
00:36:54,637 --> 00:36:56,472
It was the bloodiest street gang
in New York history.

644
00:36:57,473 --> 00:36:59,183
All Vietnamese refugees.

645
00:37:01,393 --> 00:37:03,103
I hear you guys owned Chinatown
in the '80s.

646
00:37:03,479 --> 00:37:04,939
We owned New York.

647
00:37:06,106 --> 00:37:07,775
One last story
for the record books.

648
00:37:08,526 --> 00:37:12,905
How an original member of B.T.K.
almost took the feds for 10 million.

649
00:37:14,323 --> 00:37:15,533
Give me that confession.

650
00:37:18,452 --> 00:37:19,537
Give me the pen.

651
00:37:37,805 --> 00:37:39,139
You wanted to talk to me?

652
00:37:39,682 --> 00:37:41,725
You have a sharp mind.

653
00:37:42,518 --> 00:37:43,853
And people like you.

654
00:37:44,353 --> 00:37:45,479
Some people.

655
00:37:45,938 --> 00:37:46,981
Well...

656
00:37:48,482 --> 00:37:49,817
I didn't think so before

657
00:37:49,942 --> 00:37:54,446
but I can see this arrangement
working on a permanent basis.

658
00:37:54,738 --> 00:37:56,323
I like you, too, Dennis.

659
00:37:56,991 --> 00:37:58,826
And I can see working with you
in the future

660
00:37:58,951 --> 00:38:00,703
but only on my terms,
and you know that.

661
00:38:00,995 --> 00:38:03,497
Yeah, we've been through this.
There's no deals.

662
00:38:04,164 --> 00:38:05,916
No demands, no negotiations.

663
00:38:06,041 --> 00:38:09,420
We are offering you a career,
a clean slate.

664
00:38:09,628 --> 00:38:11,672
A chance to make a difference.

665
00:38:18,971 --> 00:38:21,098
You drive a hard bargain, Dennis.

666
00:38:23,642 --> 00:38:26,395
Okay, pet leopard's off the table.
Forget about that.

667
00:38:26,854 --> 00:38:29,356
But I need the full pardon.
And the airstream.

668
00:38:29,481 --> 00:38:30,649
And I need a couch, man

669
00:38:30,774 --> 00:38:33,402
'cause everything is cold
and hard around here.

670
00:38:33,652 --> 00:38:34,695
And...

671
00:38:35,779 --> 00:38:37,364
I need a job offer for Lisbon.

672
00:38:38,741 --> 00:38:42,786
I am sure that some people
find this side of you endearing...

673
00:38:46,248 --> 00:38:47,333
but I'm not one of them.

674
00:38:50,419 --> 00:38:52,922
I'll be following
your murder trial on the TV.

675
00:38:54,465 --> 00:38:56,133
- Good luck.
- Okay.

676
00:38:56,800 --> 00:38:57,843
Thank you.

677
00:38:58,177 --> 00:38:59,678
Oh, enjoy your mail.

678
00:39:24,995 --> 00:39:27,164
Fellas. Stop right there.

679
00:39:30,834 --> 00:39:31,877
Jane...

680
00:39:33,087 --> 00:39:34,380
where did you get this from?

681
00:39:34,588 --> 00:39:37,007
Oh, a thumb drive
Gail Bertram was hiding.

682
00:39:37,883 --> 00:39:39,385
This is the first
I'm hearing about it.

683
00:39:40,344 --> 00:39:41,804
It's the first I've talked of it.

684
00:39:42,221 --> 00:39:45,766
These are all the members
of the Blake Association.

685
00:39:45,975 --> 00:39:47,017
Correct.

686
00:39:47,476 --> 00:39:50,062
I figured Bertram was keeping it
as insurance.

687
00:39:50,270 --> 00:39:52,982
Well, we've arrested or retired
all of these people.

688
00:39:53,232 --> 00:39:54,274
Yes, you have...

689
00:39:54,400 --> 00:39:56,235
Except the ones
that I've blacked out.

690
00:39:56,986 --> 00:39:58,862
Those ones you haven't caught.

691
00:40:00,531 --> 00:40:01,949
- We caught 'em all.
- Did you?

692
00:40:02,157 --> 00:40:03,784
Well, I guess Bertram was wrong then.

693
00:40:07,079 --> 00:40:08,205
Give me the names.

694
00:40:08,497 --> 00:40:11,458
A couple of judges, a congressman
several FBI agents.

695
00:40:11,583 --> 00:40:13,127
- Names.
- Big names, some of them.

696
00:40:13,794 --> 00:40:15,337
If that list ever got out
to the press

697
00:40:15,462 --> 00:40:17,673
it'd cause all kinds of turmoil,
wouldn't it?

698
00:40:19,091 --> 00:40:20,467
- Are you threatening me?
- Oh, no.

699
00:40:20,592 --> 00:40:24,221
Just pointing out that we both have
the power to create misery.

700
00:40:26,306 --> 00:40:27,349
Okay.

701
00:40:27,975 --> 00:40:31,603
You give me the names,
and we'll agree to all of your terms

702
00:40:31,729 --> 00:40:32,771
on the contract.

703
00:40:33,188 --> 00:40:35,899
If I give you the names,
you'll break the contract.

704
00:40:36,275 --> 00:40:37,943
You've already done it once.

705
00:40:38,736 --> 00:40:39,778
What do you propose?

706
00:40:39,903 --> 00:40:41,947
I propose that you surrender
to my terms

707
00:40:42,156 --> 00:40:43,907
and when I'm finished
with your contract

708
00:40:44,033 --> 00:40:45,200
and only then

709
00:40:46,326 --> 00:40:47,703
I will give you the names.

710
00:40:51,623 --> 00:40:52,791
Get him outta here.

711
00:41:12,519 --> 00:41:13,562
Well...

712
00:41:13,729 --> 00:41:17,399
You did it this time.
You brought the FBI to its knees.

713
00:41:19,485 --> 00:41:22,446
- Did I?
- And they conceded to all your demands.

714
00:41:22,571 --> 00:41:25,157
Every single one.
How did you do it?

715
00:41:25,532 --> 00:41:28,535
Well, remember that thumb drive
we found in Bertram's wine cellar?

716
00:41:28,786 --> 00:41:29,912
The encrypted database?

717
00:41:30,370 --> 00:41:32,331
It's a list
of Blake Association members.

718
00:41:32,456 --> 00:41:35,125
- I used it as leverage.
- But we never broke the encryption.

719
00:41:35,292 --> 00:41:36,752
Yeah, the FBI doesn't know that.

720
00:41:39,129 --> 00:41:42,800
That's why you escaped.
To create a bogus list of names.

721
00:41:42,925 --> 00:41:45,094
And to enjoy
a bacon-wrapped hot dog.

722
00:41:46,678 --> 00:41:49,014
I have to say, I am impressed.

723
00:41:49,139 --> 00:41:51,767
- You outplayed the FBI.
- No contest.

724
00:41:52,017 --> 00:41:53,519
Did they tell you
when I start work?

725
00:41:53,811 --> 00:41:55,020
When we start.

726
00:41:55,979 --> 00:41:57,022
We?

727
00:41:57,648 --> 00:41:59,775
- What about your job?
- Nah. It was boring.

728
00:41:59,942 --> 00:42:02,319
- Yeah, I told you.
- Yeah. But you had no right to.

729
00:42:02,444 --> 00:42:04,404
It's my life, and I'll decide
what to do with it.

730
00:42:04,530 --> 00:42:06,990
- And you decided correctly.
- There was never any choice.

731
00:42:07,116 --> 00:42:08,826
Oh, come on. It'll be fun.

732
00:42:09,243 --> 00:42:11,036
Let's see what kinda trouble
we can make.

733
00:42:11,537 --> 00:42:13,914
- I got you something.
- For me?

734
00:42:23,423 --> 00:42:24,967
You got me socks.

735
00:42:25,134 --> 00:42:26,718
Handmade in Washington.

736
00:42:28,804 --> 00:42:29,972
Oh...


